<TopicPage slug="subjects/music/foundational/sound-and-music">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Music is physics you can dance to. Every sound — a violin, a voice, a dropped saucepan — is
    air pressure oscillating: molecules crowding together and spreading apart, hundreds or
    thousands of times per second. Every musical note is an oscillation at a steady
    <strong>frequency</strong>, counted in hertz (Hz = waves per second). The international
    tuning note, A above middle C, is <strong>440 Hz</strong>: push a speaker cone in and out
    440 times a second and your eardrum, copying it, reports that note. Pitch is simply
    frequency as your ear experiences it; <strong>loudness</strong> is amplitude (how far the
    pressure swings); and <strong>timbre</strong> — what makes a violin and a flute playing the
    same note instantly recognisable — is the shape of the wave riding on top of the pure tone:
    the recipe of overtones each instrument adds.
  </p>
  <p>
    Once you see notes as numbers, the whole of music theory becomes arithmetic you can hear.
    Intervals are ratios, scales are ways of slicing the octave, chords are stacks of ratios,
    rhythm is time divided by fractions. The elements-of-music list — melody, scales, harmony,
    rhythm, tempo, texture, dynamics, articulation — is not a pile of Italian vocabulary to
    memorise; it is the consequence of one physical fact, explored below: <em>simple frequency
    ratios sound good to human ears</em>. That single discovery, attributed to Pythagoras
    experimenting with stretched strings around 550 BCE, is the foundation Western music is
    built on.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="a live tone — raise f for pitch, a for volume; the grey wave is one octave above yours" widget="plotter" config={toneCfg} />

  <p>
    Slide the frequency and count the cycles squeezed into those 20 milliseconds. Then notice
    the grey curve completes exactly two cycles for your one — that relationship, a clean 2:1,
    is the <strong>octave</strong>, the only interval every musical culture on Earth treats as
    "the same note, higher". Slide f to 440 and your own wave and its octave land on the same
    pitch class an octave apart: 440 and 880 Hz are both "A".
  </p>

  <h2>Scales and modes: slicing the octave</h2>
  <p>
    The octave contains infinitely many possible pitches, and every culture carves it up — but
    with a strong shared preference for the ratios Pythagoras found consonant. The octave
    itself is 2:1; the <strong>perfect fifth</strong> is 3:2 (in hertz: 220 → 330); the
    <strong>perfect fourth</strong> is 4:3; the <strong>major third</strong> is 5:4. A
    <strong>scale</strong> is a selection of these slices, ordered, with a home note (the
    tonic). The <strong>major scale</strong> uses a step-pattern of tones and semitones (T-T-S-
    T-T-T-S); the <strong>natural minor</strong> rearranges it (T-S-T-T-S-T-T) and sounds
    darker — the same raw material, a different emotional setting. The <strong>chromatic
    scale</strong> uses all twelve semitones; the <strong>pentatonic</strong> (five-note) scale
    drops the two most clashing steps, which is why nearly every folk tradition — Scottish
    laments, Chinese guzheng, West African kora, blues — rediscovered it independently.
  </p>
  <p>
    Beyond major and minor lie the <strong>modal</strong> colours (Dorian, Phrygian, Mixolydian
    — the same seven steps starting from a different tonic, each with a recognisable shade:
    Mixolydian's flattened seventh is the sound of rock riffs and sea shanties) and the great
    non-Western systems: the <strong>rāga</strong> of Indian classical music, a melodic
    framework with its own permitted notes, characteristic phrases and even times of day, and
    the <strong>maqām</strong> of Arabic music, whose quarter-tone intervals fall between the
    piano's keys. A piano can't play a maqām properly — a reminder that Western tuning is one
    dialect of a planetary language.
  </p>

  <h2>Equal temperament: the beautiful compromise</h2>
  <p>
    Here is a problem Pythagoras left unsolved: pure ratios don't stack into a closed circle.
    Twelve perfect fifths (multiplying by 3:2 twelve times) overshoot seven octaves (2:1 seven
    times) by about a quarter of a semitone — the famous "Pythagorean comma". Tune a keyboard
    in pure fifths and the last fifth sounds sour; worse, some keys become unusable. The
    solution, computed by Zhu Zaiyu in Ming China (1584) and adopted in Europe shortly after,
    is <strong>equal temperament</strong>: divide the octave into 12 exactly equal steps, each
    a factor of 2^(1/12) ≈ 1.0595. Every fifth is now very slightly flat (700 cents instead of
    a pure 702), but the error is spread evenly — every key sounds equally, modestly in tune.
    Bach's <em>Well-Tempered Clavier</em> (1722) toured all 24 major and minor keys to show
    the trick worked. It is a genuine trade-off: pure harmony for some keys in exchange for
    usable harmony in all of them — the reason a modern pianist can modulate anywhere.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the tuning note A = 440 Hz is a law of nature." It is a
    convention, standardised by the International Organization for Standardization (ISO 16) in
    1955 and chosen partly to suit orchestras and instruments; Baroque ensembles often tune to
    A ≈ 415 Hz, and orchestras have drifted upward for brightness. Pitch standards are
    politics with a tuning fork.
  </p>

  <h2>Tonality and harmony: notes that agree to move</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Tonality</strong> is the sense that one note — the tonic — is home, and that other
    notes create journeys away from and back to it. <strong>Harmony</strong> is what happens
    when notes sound simultaneously. The workhorse chords are triads built on the scale
    degrees: the <strong>primary chords</strong> I, IV and V (in C major: C, F, G) can
    harmonise almost any folk melody; the <strong>secondary chords</strong> (ii, iii, vi) add
    colour. The crucial spice is the <strong>dominant seventh</strong> (G-B-D-F in C): that
    added fourth note creates a dissonance that <em>demands</em> resolution to the tonic — it
    is the chord behind every "and then…?" in blues, jazz and classical music alike. Chords
    become <strong>cadences</strong>, the punctuation of music: V→I ("perfect cadence") is a
    full stop, IV→I ("plagiarised"? no — plagal) is the gentle amen, and an unresolved cadence
    is a musical cliffhanger. <strong>Tonality</strong> also covers the major/minor contrast —
    same seven notes, different third degree, opposite emotional valence — and
    <strong>modulation</strong>, changing key mid-piece, usually to a related key (five steps
    around the circle of fifths) so the ear follows. A <strong>pedal</strong> note (usually the
    tonic held underneath) creates the friction of changing harmony against a fixed floor.
  </p>

  <h2>Melody: the line you hum on the way home</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>melody</strong> is a sequence of pitches arranged in time — the part of music
    people actually remember. Its craft is in its <strong>melodic movement</strong>: steps
    (adjacent scale notes) feel calm, leaps add drama, and a good tune balances both ("Twinkle
    Twinkle" is mostly steps with one leap for lift). Melodies are built from chord shapes:
    an <strong>arpeggio</strong> spells a chord one note at a time (the sweeping sound of
    harps and heavy-metal guitar solos alike). Multiple melodies can coexist: a
    <strong>countermelody</strong> is a second, subordinate tune woven against the main one.
    Composers brand their music with recurring melodies: a <strong>leitmotif</strong> is a
    melody attached to a character or idea, transformed as the story moves (Wagner's operas,
    John Williams's shark), while a <strong>hook</strong> is the fragment engineered to
    burrow into memory and a <strong>riff</strong> is its riff-driven cousin — a repeated
    melodic figure that generates the whole song (Satisfaction, Smoke on the Water). Indian
    classical music makes this systematic: a rāga's permitted phrases <em>are</em> the melody's
    grammar.
  </p>

  <h2>Rhythm, metre and tempo: arithmetic in time</h2>
  <p>
    Pitch organises frequency; rhythm organises duration. <strong>Metre</strong> is the grid
    underneath: a time signature is literally a fraction — 4/4 means four quarter-note beats
    per bar, the pop default; 3/4 is the waltz. <strong>Simple time</strong> divides each beat
    in two; <strong>compound time</strong> (6/8, 9/8) divides it in three, giving the lilting
    feel of a jig or "House of the Rising Sun". The fun starts when the grid is defied.
    <strong>Syncopation</strong> shifts accents onto weak beats — the entire engine of funk,
    jazz and reggae. <strong>Hemiola</strong> plays 3 against 2 (the "America" from West Side
    Story); <strong>cross-rhythms</strong> and <strong>polyrhythms</strong> layer whole
    independent fractions — three against four — which resolve deliciously when the cycles
    realign; West African drumming and Hindustani <strong>tāla</strong> (cyclic rhythmic
    frameworks, some 16 beats deep, clapped and counted) carry this to virtuosic heights.
    <strong>Tempo</strong> is the speed, in beats per minute, named in Italian because Italian
    publishers dominated early printed music: largo (very slow, ~40–60 BPM) through andante
    (walking, ~76–108) to allegro (fast, ~120–156) and presto (very fast, 168+).
    <strong>Accelerando</strong> and <strong>rallentando</strong> are the pedals of time
    itself — speeding up, slowing down — and <strong>rubato</strong> ("stolen time") is the
    soloist subtly stretching and squeezing the beat for expression, paying it back later.
  </p>

  <h2>Texture: how many things are happening at once</h2>
  <p>
    Texture describes the fabric of simultaneous activity. <strong>Monophonic</strong> texture
    is one unaccompanied line — a lone chanter, a whistled tune, a crowd in <strong>unison</strong>.
    <strong>Homophonic</strong> texture — the default of pop and hymns — is melody plus chordal
    accompaniment: a singer over a guitar. <strong>Polyphonic</strong> texture is two or more
    independent melodies of equal importance, the art of Bach fugues and round ("Row, Row, Row
    Your Boat" is the gateway drug: when the second voice enters, you are in polyphony).
    <strong>Heterophonic</strong> texture, common in Javanese gamelan and Middle Eastern
    ensembles, has everyone playing the same melody but each ornamenting it differently —
    unity with shimmer. Special cases: <strong>antiphonal</strong> music alternates between
    two separated groups (call and response, Venetian choirs across the cathedral), and a
    <strong>drone</strong> — a single sustained note under everything, from bagpipes to Indian
    tanpura — is the most ancient texture of all.
  </p>

  <h2>Musical devices, dynamics and articulation</h2>
  <p>
    The remaining elements are the composer's toolkit of construction and expression. The
    structural devices are all ways of repeating with purpose: <strong>repetition</strong>
    (recall is pleasure — your brain rewards recognising patterns), <strong>sequence</strong>
    (a figure restated at a new pitch, climbing like stairs), <strong>imitation</strong> (one
    voice copying another, the basis of canon and fugue), <strong>ostinato</strong> (a
    stubbornly repeated pattern, from Pachelbel's bass line to every bass groove), and
    <strong>Alberti bass</strong> — the broken-chord accompaniment (low-high-middle-high) that
    purrs under Classical-era piano sonatas.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Dynamics</strong> are the loudness markings: pianissimo (pp, very soft) through
    piano, mezzo-piano, mezzo-forte, to forte and fortissimo (ff, very loud) — roughly a
    thousand-fold range of acoustic energy from pp to ff. <strong>Crescendo</strong> and
    <strong>diminuendo</strong> are gradual changes of level; <strong>terraced dynamics</strong> —
    abrupt whole-ensemble shifts, a Baroque signature — are their opposite. (And loudness
    itself is logarithmic to your ear: each +10 dB is roughly "twice as loud" for ten times
    the energy — whisper 30 dB, conversation 60, sustained ~85 dB and above risks hearing
    damage.) <strong>Articulation</strong> shapes individual notes: <strong>legato</strong>
    (smooth, connected) versus <strong>staccato</strong> (short, detached). The ornaments are
    rapid embellishments: a <strong>trill</strong> hammers alternation with the note above; a
    <strong>mordent</strong> flicks once to the note below; an <strong>appoggiatura</strong>
    "leans" — a dissonant grace note that steals time from the main note and resolves into it;
    an <strong>acciaccatura</strong> is "crushed", played in an instant before the beat. These
    tiny gestures are where interpretation lives: two pianists playing the same score differ
    chiefly in appoggiaturas, rubato and dynamics.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Why do simple ratios sound consonant at all? The leading explanation is
    <strong>harmonicity of the ear itself</strong>: a plucked string's overtone series already
    vibrates at 2f, 3f, 4f…, so two notes whose fundamentals lock into the same series fuse
    cleanly, while complex ratios make the inner ear register competing "virtual pitches" —
    roughness you can literally feel as beating between close overtones. This predicts
    consonance hierarchies across cultures surprisingly well, yet it cannot be the whole
    story: training shifts perception (Bali's stretched octaves, Blues bent thirds), and
    consonance preferences appear partly learned. Consonance sits at the exact frontier where
    the physics of sound meets the psychology of listening — music is applied
    pattern-recognition, and the two disciplines keep meeting in the middle.
  </p>
  <p>
    Quantitatively, the equal-temperament compromise is worth pinning down: a pure fifth is
    701.96 cents (100 cents = one semitone), tempered to 700 — an error of 2 cents, far below
    the ~5–10 cents most listeners detect in isolation, but audible in sustained chords. The
    major third fares worse: pure 5:4 is 386 cents against a tempered 400 — 14 cents flat of
    perfection, which is why choirs and string quartets that tune by ear drift sharper than
    pianos, and why 19- or 31-tone experimental tunings exist. Deeper open questions: why the
    octave is universal while the division inside it is not; why some brains (amusia) miss
    pitch relationships entirely; and whether microtonal electronic music — now trivially
    synthesised — will finally erode the piano's 300-year monopoly on the octave.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ISO 16 (standard pitch); Benson, Music: A Mathematical Offering; Zhu Zaiyu's equal-temperament calculation (1584); Sethares, Tuning, Timbre, Spectrum, Scale.</p>
</TopicPage>
